 that must just be an  excruciating reality for them and I  think increases the pressure for titles  to be held accountable absolutely as you  mentioned you know this must be so  distressing for the many we go  in the diaspora we have heard from a lot  of them on al Jazeera could this report  change things for them for their  families in the camps though I certainly  think it brings that much more pressure  to there particularly on member states  of the United Nations to push for an  independent fact-finding mission to the  region ideally under the auspices of the  High Commissioner for Human Rights you  know and for any of the governments that  have resisted the idea that Chinese  authorities are actually carrying out  this campaign of repression you know the  facade of support for China's position  really is starting to crumble and this  report goes a long way towards you know  denying people the ability to say well  it's not really happening or there's no  credible evidence here it is and how you  know much more important is it to do  this now because the report goes into  plans you know to extend restrictions on  Muslims around the country on other  minorities too well we think it's been  urging to mount that kind of  investigation for well over a year now  and I think every day that goes by  without holding China accountable  reinforces an extraordinary kind of  impunity and if anybody thinks that  those abuses are gonna stay within  China's borders we've got news for you  they're not and that's why China really  needs to come under pressure right now  to play by the same rules as every other  government in the world is expected to  and we've seen you know that the  government hasn't responded to  international pressure at all so far  they have let a few people into these  camps into these areas but they have  been so heavily monitored and their  movement restricted no it's a it is  certainly a tall order but let's imagine  for a moment that any other governmen